# The stores that are left out because no step reads their slot, where a processor keeps many registers across a call. In the arm of `clear` that calls, more values wait for what lies behind the calls than registers are kept, so some wait in slots and are reloaded for the call of `sink`. THE LIE, testing=SpillsThatStepsReadAreTakenToBeReadByNone: the allocator takes the slots that steps read to be read by none, and the encoder leaves out their stores.
# No check is asked for here, as in a build of a user: the encoder asks at each read of a slot whether its store was left out, and the build stops by name. Without that question it would end well and the code would read slots that nobody wrote.
# On x86-64 the entry of this function runs out of registers and the shape is another: its own test is a-slot-whose-store-is-left-out-though-it-is-read-stops-the-build-by-name.ir.
# f4 = clear
# -- source: a-slot-whose-store-is-left-out-though-it-is-read-where-more-values-wait-than-registers-are-kept-stops-the-build-by-name.ts
